---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is FUP on PTCL?',
    a: 'FUP, Fair Usage Policy, is a data cap on your broadband package. After you use a set amount of data in a month, PTCL reduces your speed for the rest of the billing cycle. The cap and the reduced speed vary by package, and PTCL has changed them over time, check the terms of your current package.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know if I’ve hit my FUP limit?',
    a: 'The classic sign: your speed was fine all month, then dropped sharply, often to a fraction of your package speed, and stays there. If the speed recovers on the 1st of the month, that was almost certainly FUP. Test wired at a quiet time and compare with your package speed before concluding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does PTCL Flash Fiber have a data cap?',
    a: 'PTCL’s packages have carried FUP limits, and the limits have changed over time, including a widely reported update in early 2025. Whether your specific package has a cap, and how large it is, depends on the package terms you signed up to. Check your usage and package terms in the PTCL app or customer portal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I avoid hitting the FUP limit?',
    a: 'Check your usage mid-month, set video quality to 1080p instead of 4K on secondary TVs, pause cloud backups and large downloads until off-peak, and check whether any device is uploading constantly. If you hit the cap every month, the honest fix is a package with a higher limit.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'PTCL Data Cap (FUP) Explained', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/ptcl-fup-data-cap' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PTCL Data Cap (FUP) Explained: What Happens at the Limit"
  description="PTCL FUP explained: what the data cap is, how to tell if you have hit it, how to check your usage, and what to do when your speed drops mid-month."
  canonical="/guides/ptcl-fup-data-cap"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / PTCL Data Cap (FUP)</p>
      <h1>PTCL Data Cap (FUP) Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Your PTCL was fast all month, then suddenly it wasn’t, and nothing you do
        brings it back. That pattern has a name: you’ve hit your Fair Usage Policy
        limit. Here’s what it is and what to do about it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>What is FUP?</h2>
      <p>
        FUP, Fair Usage Policy, is a data cap on your broadband package. After you
        use a set amount of data in a month, the provider reduces your speed for the
        rest of the billing cycle. The connection keeps working; it just gets much
        slower. The cap resets at the start of the next billing cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        PTCL’s packages have carried FUP limits, and the limits have changed over time
       , including a widely reported update in early 2025. The cap and the reduced
        speed vary by package, so there is no single number that applies to every PTCL
        customer. Check the terms of your current package, and be careful with
        third-party sites listing specific caps, as they go out of date quickly.
      </p>

      <h2>How to tell if you’ve hit the cap</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sudden, sustained slowdown.</strong> Your speed drops sharply, often to a small fraction of your package speed, and stays there day after day.</li>
        <li><strong>It recovers on the 1st.</strong> If the speed comes back at the start of the month, that was almost certainly FUP.</li>
        <li><strong>Everything is slow, equally.</strong> Unlike a Wi-Fi problem (one room, one device) or congestion (evenings only), FUP slows everything, all day, until the reset.</li>
        <li><strong>Your usage was heavy.</strong> 4K streaming, game downloads, cloud backups and large uploads eat data fast, a household streaming 4K daily can burn through hundreds of gigabytes in a month.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Before concluding it’s FUP, rule out the other causes: test wired at a quiet
        time (see our <a href="/guides/troubleshoot-slow-internet">troubleshooter</a>),
        and check whether the slowdown is all day or only in the evenings.
      </p>

      <h2>How to check your usage</h2>
      <p>
        Check your data usage in the PTCL app or customer portal, it shows how much
        of your allowance you’ve used in the current cycle. If the usage is at or near
        the cap for your package, that confirms it. If you can’t find the usage figure,
        call PTCL on 1218 and ask, and ask what the cap and the reduced speed are for
        your package while you’re at it.
      </p>

      <h2>What to do about it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>This month:</strong> set video quality to 1080p instead of 4K on secondary TVs, pause cloud backups and large downloads, and check whether any device is uploading constantly (a stuck backup can burn data silently).</li>
        <li><strong>Next month:</strong> check your usage mid-month so the cap doesn’t surprise you. If you’re at 80% by the 15th, ease off or accept the slowdown is coming.</li>
        <li><strong>Every month:</strong> if you hit the cap regularly, the honest fix is a package with a higher limit, or a provider whose packages don’t cap the way yours does. Compare on our <a href="/compare">comparisons page</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>If the cap wasn’t in your terms:</strong> complain to PTCL on 1218 with your package details. A cap you weren’t told about is a legitimate complaint.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How much data do common activities use?</h2>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Activity</th><th>Data used</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Netflix HD (1080p)</td><td>~3 GB per hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Netflix 4K</td><td>~7 GB per hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>YouTube HD</td><td>~1.5–3 GB per hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Video call (Zoom, 1080p)</td><td>~1.5–2.5 GB per hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Online gaming</td><td>~0.05–0.3 GB per hour (game downloads are the big cost, 50–150 GB each)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Music streaming</td><td>~0.05–0.15 GB per hour</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="small muted">
        Figures are the services’ own published estimates. Two hours of 4K Netflix a
        day uses over 400 GB a month on its own.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Is your speed actually capped?</h2>
        <p>Test wired and compare with your package, the numbers tell you for certain.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/tools/package-health">Package health checker →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
